<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Governorate</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as gubernii, governorates</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A governorate (guberniya) was the main administrative unit of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a>, headed by a governor appointed by the tsar. The system was reorganised by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catherine-the-great">Catherine the Great</a> in 1775, and it remained the basis of Russian provincial government until the revolution.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After Poltava autonomy became nominal: the territory went into the Kyiv <b>Governorate</b>, three imperial regiments were stationed permanently, Peter I's trade rules banned exports and redirected trade through northern Russia, and the Little Russian Collegium ruled from 1722 to 1727.</blockquote><a href="/empire/cossack-hetmanate">Cossack Hetmanate · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Lagash lay north-west of the junction of the Euphrates and Tigris and east of Uruk, at modern Al-Hiba in Dhi Qar <b>Governorate</b>, Iraq.</blockquote><a href="/empire/lagash">Lagash · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The 27 <b>governorates</b> answer to Cairo; Freedom House scored political rights 6 of 40 in 2024, 'Not Free'; an estimated 60,000 political prisoners are held, and Reporters Without Borders ranked Egypt 160th of 180 in 2017.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-egypt">Republic of Egypt · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>After the February Revolution of 1917 the Russian Provisional Government created the Autonomous <b>Governorate</b> of Estonia, uniting the Estonian-speaking districts of the old Estonian and Livonian governorates, and an elected provincial assembly, the Maapäev, met in Tallinn.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-estonia">Republic of Estonia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Catherine II's Provincial Reform of 1775 reorganised the empire into some 50 <b>governorates</b> (gubernii) of roughly 300,000 to 400,000 inhabitants each, administered by governors answerable to the centre, with governors-general overseeing groups of provinces.</blockquote><a href="/empire/russian-empire">Russian Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Today Sidon, or Saida, is the third-largest city of Lebanon and capital of the South <b>Governorate</b>, with some 80,000 people in the city and more than a quarter of a million in its metropolitan area; the ancient basin is a fishing port, and the tell of Phoenician Sidon…</blockquote><a href="/empire/sidon">Sidon · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
